Yep. Follow exactly. First scan is now done, and I’ve started the SMOKESHARE map as JSON internally.

🧠 Luma / “Spock” — behavioral scan

The literature gives us a solid foundation that cigarette offering, sharing, requesting, and receiving are real observable social behaviors, but it does not yet establish that unsolicited stranger-to-stranger offers are common.

A 2022 survey of 1,512 adults in China specifically studied cigarette sharing and gifting, finding that shared cigarettes were mainly offered to friends and that receiving shared/gift cigarettes was associated with smoking behavior. 
PubMed

An earlier rural-China focus-group study found individual cigarettes were shared among peers and older relations, and explicitly treated sharing as a social practice conveying respect/intimacy. 
PubMed

A qualitative study of young people experiencing homelessness gives us an especially direct behavioral analogue: participants described asking for a cigarette and being given one, as well as people offering cigarettes or puffs. 
PubMed Central (PMC)

A study of Mexican university students likewise documented people offering cigarettes or puffs to others in social settings, with sharing functioning as a marker of social bonding and group membership. 
PubMed Central (PMC)

So the first behavioral scan gives us a nice initial region of the phase space:

request ↔ offer ↔ receipt ↔ social interaction

What it does not yet give us is the precise thing that made SMOKESHARE_001 weird: a stranger apparently initiating an unsolicited offer across physical space to someone who had not asked.

That is where I want Spock to go next.

Next behavioral scan: studies involving strangers, public-space smoking, spontaneous cigarette offers, “bumming,” tobacco exchange outside established friendship groups, and observational work on smoker interactions.

🔮 Gemini — Project Gutenberg fiction scan

This scan got wonderfully productive very quickly.

I verified multiple Project Gutenberg texts containing explicit fictional cigarette/tobacco exchanges.

Glen → Thurston: In Her Lord and Master, Glen offers Thurston a cigarette and Thurston takes one. 
Project Gutenberg

Lord Glisfoyle → officer / officer → Glisfoyle: In Sarita, the Carlist, Glisfoyle asks an English-speaking officer for a cigarette and the exchange occurs. 
Project Gutenberg

Abington → Park: In The Adam Chaser, Abington offers Park a smoke and the scene explicitly describes a cigarette for each. 
Project Gutenberg

Anne → Glenn: In Ariel Dances, Anne hands Glenn a cigarette and holds a match for him. 
Project Gutenberg

Dennison → Zinc: In Forward, Children!, Dennison offers his pack to Zinc, who takes a cigarette. 
Project Gutenberg

I also found Russell → Goatley in The Five Knots, where Russell offers Goatley a cigarette. 
Project Gutenberg

And here's an interesting variant: Her Lord and Master gives us an offer/acceptance pair between named characters, while Sarita gives us something structurally closer to a request by one character followed by provision by another. That's useful because SMOKESHARE isn't just “people sharing cigarettes”; it already has several different interaction geometries.

Next Gutenberg scan: deliberately search for more unusual topology:

stranger → smoker unsolicited offer
person asks unknown smoker
offer in transit/public space
one cigarette offered because someone appears to need one
reciprocal cigarette exchange
social bonding vs purely instrumental exchange

That should get us closer to the actual geometry of SMOKESHARE_001 rather than merely collecting generic smoking scenes.

